1. The Property (Padding vs. Margin)
  p = Padding: Space inside the element (pushes content away from the edges).
  m = Margin: Space outside the element (pushes the whole element away from other things).

2. The Direction
The second letter tells Tailwind exactly where to put that space:
Letter   |   Meaning   |   Axis/Direction   |   Example
x            x-axis         Left & Right         px-4
y            y-axis         Top & Bottom         py-4
t             Top           Top only             mt-4
b            Bottom        Bottom only           pb-4
l            Left          Left only             ml-4
r           Right          Right only            pr-4
(none)    All sides        Top, Bottom,          p-4
                          Left,Right
p-4 = Padding on ALL 4 sides (inside the box).
m-4 = Margin on ALL 4 sides (outside the box).

pt-4 = Padding Top only.
my-4 = Margin Top and Bottom.
m-4 = Margin everywhere.

The Number Scale
The numbers at the end (like px-4 or py-3.5) represent a specific size in standard CSS pixels.
Tailwind uses a multiple of 4 pixels for its base scale:

p-1 = 4px
p-2 = 8px
p-3 = 12px
p-4 = 16px
p-5 = 20px
p-3.5 = 14px (Tailwind added half-sizes for fine-tuning

So, when you wrote py-3.5 px-5 in your table cell earlier,
you were telling it to have 14 pixels of padding on the top/bottom,
and 20 pixels of padding on the left/right!














